**Earnings beat and margin expansion** – Adjusted EPS of $0.78 was up 15% YoY and also topped guidance. Operating margin improved to 19.2%, a 90‑bp lift year‑over‑year, showing the business is not just growing but doing so profitably.  

**Segment performance** – The Life‑Science‑Diagnostics‑Applied (LDA) franchise, which will become the stand‑alone Agilent after the Keysight spin‑off, delivered 6% revenue growth (5% core) and a 19% operating margin, up 50 bps YoY. Within LDA, high‑growth pockets (Environmental +16%, Forensics +12%, Pharma/Biotech +8%) offset modest or flat areas (Chemical/Energy +1%, China‑related headwinds).  

**Margin leaders** – The Chemical Analysis Group posted >23% margin, up 180 bps YoY, and the Life‑Science & Diagnostics Group saw a 260 bps QoQ margin improvement, underscoring strong pricing power and cost‑discipline.  

**Product pipeline & brand strength** – Recent launches (5100 ICP‑OES, 7010 GC Triple‑Quad, award‑winning Cary 700 spectrophotometer) reinforce a differentiated portfolio and give management concrete growth levers. The Merck partnership for a companion‑diagnostics device adds a high‑margin, high‑visibility opportunity.  

**Guidance outlook** – Management projects Q4 LDA revenue of $1.07‑$1.09 bn (≈6% core growth) with a 21.1% operating margin—an improvement over the current 19% level. Full‑year LDA revenue guidance (≈$4.08 bn) and a 19% margin target suggest continued mid‑single‑digit growth and margin expansion.  

**Strategic catalyst – Keysight spin‑off** – The ongoing separation of the electronic‑measurement business (Keysight) is progressing on schedule. Once completed, Agilent will be a pure‑play analytical‑lab/diagnostics company, which historically trades at a premium to diversified peers. The spin‑off should unlock additional valuation upside for both entities.  

**Risk considerations** – The only notable headwinds are modest growth in China‑related segments and a flat Chemical/Energy line, but these are offset by strong performance elsewhere and are unlikely to derail the overall trajectory. No guidance cuts or earnings misses were reported.  

**Conclusion** – The combination of revenue beat, accelerating earnings, expanding margins, a robust product pipeline, and a clear strategic spin‑off makes Agilent’s stock appear undervalued relative to its growth prospects. The balance of evidence points to continued upside, supporting a **Buy** recommendation.
