**Operational outlook** – The airline will continue its fleet‑replacement program (55 main‑line + 38 regional deliveries this year) but has already deferred five 787 deliveries, cutting 2016 wide‑body capacity by ~2.5 %. This signals a cautious stance on capacity growth amid a still‑soft demand environment.  

**Bottom line** – The Q1 2015 earnings beat is primarily a one‑off boost from dramatically lower fuel costs and the exclusion of special items. Underlying revenue trends are negative, and cost pressures from labor contracts are rising. With a solid cash position but uncertain forward‑looking operating dynamics, the stock does not merit a clear‑cut buy, nor is there a compelling downside trigger to justify a sell at this point.  

**Recommendation** – Hold the position while monitoring: (1) fuel price trajectory, (2) the impact of higher labor costs on CASM, and (3) any improvement in passenger yields or cargo demand in the coming quarters.
