Rationale:  

**Top‑line momentum** – Revenue jumped 17% YoY to $960 M, driven by a 22% surge in new‑vehicle sales and a 21% rise in used‑vehicle sales. Unit volumes are up 15% across the board, with especially strong gains in the Southeast and in luxury/import segments.  

**Earnings acceleration** – Diluted EPS from continuing operations rose from $0.08 to $0.27 (a >200% increase) and GAAP EPS climbed to $0.22 from $0.01 a year ago. The earnings boost stems from a 12% rise in gross profit and a 370‑bp reduction in SG&A as a share of gross profit.  

**Margin dynamics** – Although the overall gross‑profit margin slipped 70 bps to 17% (reflecting a higher mix of lower‑margin new‑vehicle sales), the company is still generating healthy margins: new‑vehicle margin at 7.1% (up 60 bps) and used‑vehicle margin above 11%. SG&A efficiency improved, with same‑store SG&A falling to 79.3% of gross profit from 83%.  

**Cost discipline & capex** – SG&A expense as a percent of gross profit is trending down, fixed‑cost reductions from restructuring and lower insurance costs are evident, and capex remains disciplined at roughly $25 M (in line with depreciation).  

**Liquidity & balance‑sheet health** – As of March 31, total liquidity stood at ~$203 M (cash $29 M plus $104 M of undrawn revolving credit). The company has no material debt maturities until September 2012, and it is compliant with all covenants. The board’s $30 M debt‑repurchase authorization provides additional flexibility.  

**Operational tailwinds** – Strong consumer confidence, improved lending standards, and aggressive manufacturer incentives have boosted unit sales. Inventory management is tight (≈32 days supply, down from prior periods), and F&I revenue is up 27% thanks to higher per‑vehicle fees and higher unit volume.  

**Risks / caveats** –  
- Gross‑margin compression from a higher share of lower‑margin new‑vehicle sales.  
- A modest, ongoing loss from a discontinued California store ($1.5 M) and an expected $0.01‑$0.02 EPS hit each quarter from vacated‑property costs.  
- Effective tax rate remains high (38‑40%).  
- The business is still somewhat dependent on manufacturer incentives and a favorable credit environment.  

**Conclusion** – The earnings beat, robust revenue and earnings growth, improving cost structure, and solid liquidity outweigh the modest margin pressure and one‑off store loss. Assuming the current market price does not already fully price in this acceleration, the stock appears undervalued relative to its growth trajectory. Therefore, a **Buy** recommendation is warranted.
